/**
 * `/app/design` — the reader's design system, rendered by the reader (ADR-0053).
 *
 * Every specimen below is the *component*, imported and given data, or the
 * output of the library function that builds the markup on the pages that use
 * it. Nothing here is a hand-written likeness of something else on the site:
 * a likeness drifts, and a style guide that has drifted is worse than none,
 * because it is believed.
 *
 * That is what makes this page a regression surface rather than a gallery.
 * `make shots` renders it at four widths and fails on horizontal overflow, the
 * axe matrix scans it in both themes and under forced colours (ADR-0039), and
 * `tests/e2e/design.spec.ts` compares the contrast ratios it computes against
 * `docs/verification/contrast.json`. A component that is not on this page is
 * covered by none of that.
 *
 * ## It reaches nothing
 *
 * No API call, no database, no release point. The page renders identically on
 * an empty machine and on the deployed box, which is what lets the screenshot
 * and accessibility suites treat it as a fixed target — every other route's
 * appearance depends on what the corpus happens to hold.
 *
 * `ClassificationLookup` is here in full, script and all, because it asks the
 * API only on input and nothing on this page types into it (ADR-0067). It is
 * the one island whose no-data property is a property of *when* it fetches
 * rather than of what it renders, so `design.spec.ts`'s request assertion is
 * what holds the claim up.
 *
 * The cost of that is the specimen text below: it is a specimen, under title 0,
 * which OLRC does not publish. Its citations and links therefore resolve to
 * nothing, and the page says so rather than borrowing a real provision's
 * identifier for words the Code does not contain.
 *
 * ## The colour table computes itself
 *
 * The pairs come from `src/data/color-pairs.json`, which `scripts/contrast.py`
 * reads as well; the ratios are computed in the browser from the tokens the
 * page has actually resolved. Two things follow. The table is right in both
 * themes without a second palette written out anywhere, and it reports what is
 * painted rather than what `site.scss` declares — the difference ADR-0042 was
 * written about, where a USWDS component ignored the token and no audit of the
 * token could see it.
 */
