  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ text */}
  <h2 class="toc-group" id="text">Statutory text and the copy control</h2>

  <p>
    Five things share the reading column, and the specimen below carries all of them:
    <strong>operative text</strong> in Spectral; <strong>quoted amending text</strong>, also in
    Spectral because it is also statutory text, on a panel labelled <em>Quoted</em>;
    <strong>editorial notes</strong> and the <strong>source credit</strong> in Archivo, the
    face the site writes <em>about</em> the law in; and a <strong>table</strong> in Archivo with
    tabular figures, inside a box that scrolls sideways and takes a tab stop so a keyboard can
    reach the scroll.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>subsection ladder</strong> — (a), (1), (A), (i), (I) — is one step of
    <code>--indent-step</code> per level, cumulative through the nesting, with the number
    hanging into the step so that the numbers at one depth line up with the text at the depth
    above. The step is <code>1.5em</code> of the reading text, <code>1em</code> below 40em; a
    designator wider than the step pushes the words beside it along that line rather than
    wrapping them under itself. The source writes its own indentation on the same elements and
    it is overridden here, so that one scale answers the question. The ladder goes seven deep in the corpus and 91.8% of sections
    stop at three (<code>docs/verification/ladder.json</code>, from
    <code>scripts/ladder.py</code>).
  </p>

  <p>
    The copy control sits above the text with a button in the gutter beside every provision.
    The controls appear only when scripting is available, since there is no other way to write
    to a clipboard. Subsection (b)(1) is highlighted the way a provision named in the URL is.
  </p>

